WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:11.120
Suppose you're digging through your grandma's attic and come across a mysterious locked suitcase.

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:17.300
Grandma tells you that the key for the suitcase is probably in this other box.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:25.960
This box contains more boxes with more boxes inside those boxes. The key is in a box somewhere.

00:00:27.039 --> 00:00:29.729
What's your algorithm to search for the key?

00:00:30.369 --> 00:00:37.288
In this unit you'll learn about recursion. Recursion is used in many algorithms, and is a building block for

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.960
understanding later units in this course.

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:47.009
Recursion can be used to solve the previous problem of searching for a key in nested boxes.

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:54.780
You will also learn to break down a problem into a base case and a recursive case.

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:57.689
I will be showing you a lot of code examples.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:01.079
I highly recommend trying to run the code for yourself.

00:01:01.539 --> 00:01:02.170
Also,

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:07.110
At least once you should try to step through a recursive function with pen and paper

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:13.260
to get a better sense of how recursive functions work. This unit also includes a lot of pseudocode.

00:01:13.930 --> 00:01:18.720
Pseudocode is a high-level description of the problem you're trying to solve in code.

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:24.270
It's written like code, but it's meant to be closer to human speech.

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:29.999
Let's get back to searching for a key in a box that contains boxes which contain other boxes.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:35.429
First, I'll show you a method to solve this problem that uses while loops.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.740
Then, I'll show you a method that uses recursion.

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:44.940
Here's the first method. 1- Make a pile of boxes to look through.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:49.800
2- While the pile is empty, grab a box and look through it.

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:53.860
3- If you find a key, you're done!

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:57.900
4- If you find a box, add it to the pile of boxes to look through later.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:00.540
5- Repeat.

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:04.680
Here's the second method. 1- Look through the box.

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:08.039
2- If you find a key, you're done!

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:11.940
3- If you find a box, go to Step 1.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:18.100
Which approach seems easier to you? The first way uses a while loop.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:22.120
Here's the first method using pseudocode.

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:27.120
First, we make a pile of boxes to look through, from the boxes in the main box.

00:02:27.819 --> 00:02:31.708
While the pile is not empty, choose a box to look through.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:38.120
For each item in the box, check if the item is another box. If it is another box,

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:41.100
append, or add it to the pile of boxes

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:47.600
we still have to look through. If the item we found is a key, then yell to everyone excitedly, "found the key!"

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:51.300
Keep checking the boxes until the pile is empty.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:56.789
The second way uses recursion. Recursion is where a function calls itself.

00:02:57.730 --> 00:02:59.820
Here's the second way in pseudocode.

00:03:00.519 --> 00:03:06.569
First, we check the box passed into the function, and go through each item in the box.

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:11.680
If the item is a box, then we pass the box into the function again.

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:16.960
If the item is a key then yell to everyone excitedly, "found the key!"

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:22.800
Both approaches accomplish the same thing, but the second approach seems cleaner.

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:26.000
A recursion is used when it makes the solution clearer.

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:33.900
There's actually no performance benefit to using recursion- in fact, loops are sometimes better for performance.

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:41.250
However, people still use recursion since it often makes code easier to understand and look cleaner.

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:47.489
Many important algorithms use recursion, so it's important to understand.

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:59.760
Because a recursive function calls itself, it's easy to write a function incorrectly, that ends up in an infinite loop.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:05.740
For example, suppose you want to write a function that prints a countdown.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:10.180
You could write it recursively, like this:

00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:17.529
However, if you run this code, there's a problem. The function will run forever.

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:22.569
If your code is in an infinite loop, press control+C to kill the script.

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:27.130
When you write a recursive function, you have to tell it when to stop recursing.

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:34.270
That's why every recursive function has two parts: the base case and the recursive case.

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:42.399
The Recursive case is when the function calls itself. The base case is when the function doesn't call itself again,

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:48.969
so it doesn't go into an infinite loop. Here is the function from before, with the addition of a base case.

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:53.590
The "if i is less than or equal to 1", is the base case.

00:04:54.220 --> 00:05:00.540
That is when the function will not call itself anymore. In the "else" section is the recursive case.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:05.640
That is what will keep happening if the base case has not yet been reached.

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:10.700
So, unlike the last example, this function will actually end.

